WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.580
Okay, I am here in Northern California

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:06.200
at home where objects like
this air quality meter,

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:09.990
and this respirator have sadly become

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:12.657
pretty common household objects.

00:00:12.657 --> 00:00:15.810
The fires have been raging in California

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:17.930
and the West more broadly for many weeks

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:20.840
and destroying homes and people's lives,

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:24.180
and ruining the air
quality for the rest of us

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:26.250
in the process, which is what led me

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:28.003
to the company we're
gonna talk about today,

00:00:28.003 --> 00:00:30.520
which is called SALO Sciences.

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:34.360
The CEO is a gentlemen named David Marvin.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:35.740
What this company is trying to do,

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:38.350
is apply satellite imagery,

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:41.000
computer vision technology and algorithms,

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:45.250
and just hardcore cloud
computing, data crunching

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:48.940
to give us a much better
idea of the health

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:53.100
of our forests, and whether
they may pose a fire risk

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:54.410
in specific regions.

00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:57.160
In the bigger picture, the
idea is that this technology

00:00:57.160 --> 00:01:01.180
could be used to really take
care of our forest better,

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:03.800
and reduce the risks of fires,

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:07.500
and other climate problems moving forward.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:10.770
So with that, let's head to SALO Sciences,

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:13.713
and figure out how their technology works.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:19.125
Well tell me a little
bit about the background

00:01:19.125 --> 00:01:21.990
of this company, and
what you guys are up to.

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:25.530
So SALO Sciences is a
conservation technology company.

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:27.660
My co-founder Chris Anderson and I,

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:29.270
we both come from academia.

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:31.200
We're both forest ecologists by training,

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:33.930
and we both met at a lab where we saw

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:37.730
the need to be bringing cutting
edge technology and science

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:40.270
out of the academic realm
and operationalizing it

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:44.533
for organizations that
are working to scale up

00:01:44.533 --> 00:01:47.480
what's called the nature based
solutions to climate change.

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:51.120
And so these are activities
like reforestation,

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:54.060
or forest restoration, land conservation,

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:56.500
really anything that leaves more carbon

00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:57.710
on the earth's landscape

00:01:57.710 --> 00:01:59.660
than lets it back into the atmosphere.

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:01.690
We're trying to build
the tools and solutions

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:04.399
that let governments, or non-profits,

00:02:04.399 --> 00:02:06.972
or even corporations,

00:02:06.972 --> 00:02:11.781
we're trying to implement
these solutions at scale

00:02:11.781 --> 00:02:14.640
that can really help make
a meaningful difference

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:16.323
in the fight against climate change.

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:23.210
So we just launched the forest
observatory a month ago.

00:02:23.210 --> 00:02:25.960
This is our first trip
to the field to do this,

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:28.500
and we'll want to come
out here much more times

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:29.740
over the next year,

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:31.068
across different parts of California,

00:02:31.068 --> 00:02:32.593
to do the validation.

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:36.950
What you're seeing here
is US Forest Service land

00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:40.900
in the Tahoe National Forest
that was thinned recently.

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:43.920
If you were to instead leave
it in its previous state,

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.050
where you had dense trees
growing near each other,

00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:50.450
much smaller, branches
much lower to the ground,

00:02:50.450 --> 00:02:52.140
what would happen as
a fire burned through,

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:54.770
it could begin catching
on the lower branches,

00:02:54.770 --> 00:02:56.290
catching on the smaller trees,

00:02:56.290 --> 00:02:58.457
and begin laddering up into
the canopy of these trees,

00:02:58.457 --> 00:02:59.681
and once that happens,

00:02:59.681 --> 00:03:02.164
they can, the fire can
jump from crown to crown,

00:03:02.164 --> 00:03:06.482
and become catastrophic,
killing off a lot of the trees

00:03:06.482 --> 00:03:09.483
and causing a lot of damage to the forest.

00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:14.481
This is a really dense forest stand

00:03:14.481 --> 00:03:19.481
that probably pre-thinning
would be this entire area.

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:24.250
So the trees are much closer together.

00:03:24.250 --> 00:03:26.840
They're smaller in
diameter for the most part.

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:29.690
You see a lot of understory buildup of the

00:03:29.690 --> 00:03:30.880
what we call ladder fuels.

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:33.300
What we're trying to do
with the forest observatory

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:36.211
is be able to map over the entire state

00:03:36.211 --> 00:03:38.380
all of the areas that are still

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:41.950
in this kind of really
dense, highly stocked state.

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:45.100
From there, the Forest
Service, State of California,

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:48.710
part landowners, can begin
planning how to restore

00:03:48.710 --> 00:03:51.813
the forest back to a fire resilient state.

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:00.430
You know one of the
areas I've been tracking

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:03.460
for a long time, or for
a decent amount of time,

00:04:03.460 --> 00:04:05.410
is using satellite imagery

00:04:05.410 --> 00:04:08.360
as sort of like this
accounting system of the earth.

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:11.020
You know, we can track deforestation now,

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:13.090
we can like literally kind of make counts

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:15.100
of all the trees on the planet,

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:16.930
and how these things are changing.

00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:18.830
I know you guys use some of these tools,

00:04:18.830 --> 00:04:21.780
but I mean, I have to assume
for this type of work,

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:25.171
the advances in technology
that have taken place

00:04:25.171 --> 00:04:27.420
over the last five years is kind of like

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:28.832
what's opening up this opportunity

00:04:28.832 --> 00:04:32.090
to modernize how this
type of stuff gets done?

00:04:32.090 --> 00:04:35.140
We see there's three key advances

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:36.840
in the last, you know, decade or so

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:39.550
that has really allowed
us to exist as a company,

00:04:39.550 --> 00:04:43.460
and a lot of really
amazing scientists do a lot

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:46.470
of great work at the
scale of the entire earth.

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:49.540
These are the advent of
satellite constellations,

00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:52.110
those like Planet Labs and Capella Space

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:53.380
that are launching these, you know,

00:04:53.380 --> 00:04:56.250
really amazing high res
sensors that capture

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:58.180
the entire surface of the earth every day,

00:04:58.180 --> 00:05:01.090
combined with a really
cheap cloud computing.

00:05:01.090 --> 00:05:04.880
So to process the insane
amounts of data coming down

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:08.340
from space every day, you got
to have a lot of computers,

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:10.102
and you've got to be
pretty cheap to run those.

00:05:10.102 --> 00:05:12.390
And then the third major advance

00:05:12.390 --> 00:05:14.330
is really artificial intelligence,

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:16.950
and the different kind of branches of AI

00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:17.960
that are now out there.

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:20.480
We're mostly working with
what's called deep learning,

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:23.200
and this is a really great
technology that allows you

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:27.700
to extract really subtle complex
multi-dimensional patterns

00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:29.280
from these satellite images,

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:31.860
and turn them into information
that's really valuable.

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:35.510
In our case, this is patterns
of ecological attributes,

00:05:35.510 --> 00:05:37.560
or ecological change, that we can use

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:39.810
to understand our earth
in a much better way.

00:05:41.581 --> 00:05:43.890
And just to kind of help
make this clear for people,

00:05:43.890 --> 00:05:46.720
I mean, give me like one or
two very specific examples

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:48.780
of projects that you guys have done.

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:51.770
We just released our first major product.

00:05:51.770 --> 00:05:54.460
This is called the California
Forest Observatory.

00:05:54.460 --> 00:05:56.780
And so this is a system we built

00:05:56.780 --> 00:05:58.400
with all those tools I just mentioned,

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:00.900
you know, cloud computing,
high res satellite imagery,

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:03.510
and deep learning to be able to map all

00:06:03.510 --> 00:06:06.050
of California's lands in high resolution,

00:06:06.050 --> 00:06:08.570
in the current year,
and do this over time,

00:06:08.570 --> 00:06:12.230
so we can begin monitoring
how the landscapes

00:06:12.230 --> 00:06:13.237
of California are changing.

00:06:13.237 --> 00:06:16.690
And we did this specifically
in the context of wildfire.

00:06:16.690 --> 00:06:19.750
So California, as many folks are aware,

00:06:19.750 --> 00:06:23.690
is going through an extraordinary
wildfire season right now.

00:06:23.690 --> 00:06:24.960
We're just halfway through.

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:27.840
We've already burned more
than 4 million acres of land.

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:32.490
Now, not all of that is
destructive and catastrophic fire.

00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:34.740
A lot of that is fire we need
to have on the landscape,

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:36.820
but there are a lot of areas that

00:06:36.820 --> 00:06:39.027
have experienced catastrophic mega fires

00:06:39.027 --> 00:06:42.227
that have burned homes,
destroyed communities.

00:06:42.227 --> 00:06:45.177
We're trying to be able to build a system,

00:06:45.177 --> 00:06:48.370
and we have at least a system
that lets us understand

00:06:48.370 --> 00:06:50.800
the fuels buildup across California

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:53.850
to get a better sense of
where future wildfires

00:06:53.850 --> 00:06:54.990
might get out of control.

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:56.030
And more importantly,

00:06:56.030 --> 00:06:58.310
where to go and do the restoration work

00:06:58.310 --> 00:07:00.860
across California to reduce the potential

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:04.140
for those wildfires to
make a catastrophic impact

00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:06.960
on our natural resources, and
our communities of the state.

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:08.600
If you try to build one of these models,

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:11.010
I mean, so you guys have
focused on California so far,

00:07:11.010 --> 00:07:13.460
so if like Australia
comes to you and they say,

00:07:13.460 --> 00:07:14.720
we want a model, you know,

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:17.660
for the entire country, what
would that entail for you guys,

00:07:17.660 --> 00:07:19.950
and like how long would
it take to build something

00:07:19.950 --> 00:07:21.540
that was fairly detailed?

00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:23.780
One of the great things
about this technology,

00:07:23.780 --> 00:07:26.810
deep learning, it takes a
lot of time, and energy,

00:07:26.810 --> 00:07:30.130
and money to be able to
train these algorithms

00:07:30.130 --> 00:07:31.850
to the point where they
can recognize things

00:07:31.850 --> 00:07:34.060
like the height of trees
in satellite images.

00:07:34.060 --> 00:07:36.729
Once you've done that, it's
learned the basic patterns

00:07:36.729 --> 00:07:38.470
in the satellite imagery

00:07:38.470 --> 00:07:40.920
that can relate to the
height of that tree,

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:45.140
and there isn't such variation
in trees and in imagery

00:07:45.140 --> 00:07:47.650
to the point where if you
were to take a trained model

00:07:47.650 --> 00:07:50.550
in California and apply it
to a forest in Australia,

00:07:50.550 --> 00:07:52.840
it will start predicting
like negative tree height

00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:53.673
or something, right?

00:07:53.673 --> 00:07:55.130
You know, it's gonna generally recognize

00:07:55.130 --> 00:07:56.460
what it means to be a tall tree,

00:07:56.460 --> 00:07:57.680
so all you have to do at that point

00:07:57.680 --> 00:07:59.987
is then tweak it just a little bit more,

00:07:59.987 --> 00:08:03.225
transfer the knowledge it
took out in California,

00:08:03.225 --> 00:08:05.950
and tweak it to work in Australia.

00:08:05.950 --> 00:08:08.300
It still seems like
you're optimistic about,

00:08:08.300 --> 00:08:10.446
you know, that this sort
of technology actually

00:08:10.446 --> 00:08:13.478
can make a significant difference,

00:08:13.478 --> 00:08:16.780
not just like in 50, 100 years,

00:08:16.780 --> 00:08:18.790
I mean, the way you were
talking, it sounds like,

00:08:18.790 --> 00:08:20.500
I mean, we could actually
use this practically

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:21.810
in the, you know, the next two,

00:08:21.810 --> 00:08:25.340
five, 10 years to have some
significant effect on things.

00:08:25.340 --> 00:08:27.870
So yes, I'm optimistic that, you know,

00:08:27.870 --> 00:08:29.217
the technology can play a role,

00:08:29.217 --> 00:08:31.440
but it really doesn't work

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:36.440
without the policy to
implement the technology.

00:08:36.620 --> 00:08:38.510
It doesn't work without
the funding that we need

00:08:38.510 --> 00:08:40.580
to do the actual work on the ground.

00:08:40.580 --> 00:08:42.286
You know, just because we're experiencing

00:08:42.286 --> 00:08:44.250
a once in a lifetime pandemic

00:08:44.250 --> 00:08:46.231
that, you know, it was
destroying a lot of budgets

00:08:46.231 --> 00:08:47.340
around the country,

00:08:47.340 --> 00:08:49.490
doesn't mean we don't have to still think

00:08:49.490 --> 00:08:51.380
about the larger problem
of climate change.

00:08:51.380 --> 00:08:52.440
That isn't going away,

00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:55.137
and as we saw from the
decline in emissions

00:08:55.137 --> 00:08:57.290
during the early parts of the lockdown,

00:08:57.290 --> 00:08:59.610
even stopping driving cars,

00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:01.290
and not going to work anymore,

00:09:01.290 --> 00:09:03.060
only caused a small blip in our emissions.

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:04.990
There's much bigger systemic changes

00:09:04.990 --> 00:09:06.890
that have to occur globally

00:09:06.890 --> 00:09:09.400
for us to really tackle this issue,

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:11.051
and it's not gonna be a simple decision

00:09:11.051 --> 00:09:12.811
that we can each take.

00:09:12.811 --> 00:09:15.430
It's gonna be a collective decision

00:09:15.430 --> 00:09:18.740
across nations, across communities,

00:09:18.740 --> 00:09:21.550
and we've got to start
working on that now,

00:09:21.550 --> 00:09:22.950
but I do think we can do it.

